@namespace Bit.BlazorUI

@* Before anything is painted: re-resolves bit-theme from localStorage / the preference cookie, which
   is what covers everything the server could not - following the OS, and a store newer than the
   cookie the response was rendered from. *@
@((MarkupString)BitThemeSsr.BuildInlineHeadScript(Nonce))

@if (EmitThemeColor)
{
    @* The browser paints its chrome from this tag while the document is still being parsed, so the
       color has to be a literal - no stylesheet has loaded yet. From the first frame the app can
       compute anything, bit-theme-color-meta takes over and re-reads the live palette (which is what
       brings an app's own token overrides and a picked accent into the chrome). *@
    <meta name="theme-color" content="@ResolveThemeColor()" />

    @if (ResolvedTheme is null)
    {
        @* This visitor follows the OS, which only the client can resolve - and the script above just
           did, onto <html>. Read it back while the browser is still parsing <head> and correct the
           guess above. Rewrites that one tag rather than adding a media-qualified pair, so there is a
           single tag for bit-theme-color-meta to keep current afterwards. *@
        @((MarkupString)BuildThemeColorCorrectionScript())
    }
}
